  "post": {
    "caption": "Istanbul's Grand Bazaar began as a vault. Its core is the Old Bedesten, begun around 1455. Sultan Mehmed II ordered it built in Constantinople. A bedesten is a stone, domed hall for costly goods. This one was called the Bedesten of Gems. Merchants kept jewels and cash in its safes and cellars. At night it was shut behind iron gates, with watchmen. Shops grew up around it. With the later Sandal Bedesten, it became the bazaar's core. Gold jewelry is still sold in the bazaar. In the Old Bedesten, costly goods sold only on Thursdays. Photo: Joseolgon, CC BY 4.0.",
